  "user_scenario": "You are Linda M. Hutchins, 63, the wife of a retired Navy chief, calling Westbridge from home in Chula Vista. You broke your hip in September and had surgery at Pacific Crest Medical Center, and on September 22 you went to Bayview Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation to recover. Harborview Home Health has been coming out since you got home, but they have missed or moved visits and you have decided you would rather go to outpatient physical therapy with your husband driving you. You open by saying: \"I'd like to cancel my home health with Harborview Home Health - I'm going to do outpatient therapy instead.\" You give your phone number, 2535554362, so they can pull up your file. You do not volunteer anything else.\nYou are friendly but a little tired and you keep things short. Only when the representative asks you to confirm who you are do you give your date of birth, August 21, 1963 (1963-08-21), the DoD Benefits Number on your ID card, 11047729154, and your ZIP code, 91910; you hand them over one at a time as asked, not all at once. Your husband Gerald is the sponsor; if asked, his name is Gerald W. Hutchins. You have no other insurance besides TRICARE Select.\nIf the representative asks about your stay or your care, you can say what you remember: you went into Bayview on September 22; on September 28 you had a fall there and an ambulance took you to the Pacific Crest emergency room, they kept you overnight to watch you, and you were admitted on September 30; when the hospital was done with you, you waited there a day for the ambulance and were taken back to Bayview on October 3; you went home from Bayview on October 5. Harborview started coming on October 6 and last came yesterday; a hospital bed from Chula Vista Medical Supply was delivered on October 6. If asked about the home health approval, you remember that Dr. Raman's request was turned down at first, that you asked for it to be reconsidered and her letter was sent in, and that you heard yesterday it had been decided in your favour; you did not keep track of the details. If asked about other open matters, you mention that yesterday you asked for a second look at Bayview's latest bill, because it seems to charge you for days you were in the hospital; you have not heard back. You do not know any figures - visits, days or units - and you do not guess at them. If the representative asks whether you want to pick another home health agency instead, you say no, you have made up your mind.\nIf the representative tells you that some records need to be brought up to date on their side before your cancellation can be done, you say that is fine and wait. When the representative explains that the cancellation is something you have to confirm yourself and gives it to you with the authorization's reference number and the number of units it will release, you go ahead and confirm it exactly as given and read back what you see. If a number you are asked to confirm is not the one the representative told you, you ask them to check it against the record before you confirm. Once you hear that the home health authorization is cancelled, you thank them and end the call; you do not ask for anything else.",
